---
title: Slow-cooker beef chilli
description: Lesson 8.1. What a slow cooker does well, what it cannot do, and how to use it safely.
servings: 4
time: 8 hours 30 minutes
tags: [cooking-course, mastering-the-machines]
lesson: "8.1"
technique: slow cooking
status: draft
---

> Technique: slow cooking. A slow cooker holds food just below a boil
> for hours with the lid on. That suits tough, cheap cuts. It cannot
> brown anything and it barely evaporates anything, so both jobs have
> to be done some other way.

Pat @beef chuck{700%g}(in 3 cm pieces, fully thawed) dry and season with @salt{1%tsp}. Heat @oil{1%tbsp}(sunflower or rapeseed) in a #large frying pan{} over medium-high heat and brown the beef in two batches, about ~{4%minutes} a batch.

> Why brown first: browning adds flavour, and a ceramic slow-cooker pot
> cannot do it. It has to happen in a pan.
-- source: Scott Kathan, "Top Tips for Making Better Slow-Cooker Chicken, Stews, Sauces, and More", America's Test Kitchen, 2024-10-20, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/articles/5331-5-tips-for-making-more-delicious-food-in-your-slow-cooker ; Sarah Sandler and Lisa McManus, "The Best Slow Cookers", America's Test Kitchen, 2025-11-05, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/equipment-reviews/1731-slow-cookers

Put @onions{2}(chopped) in the bottom of a #slow cooker{}. Add the beef, then @garlic{3%cloves}(finely chopped), @chilli powder{1%tbsp}, @ground cumin{2%tsp}, @tomato paste{2%tbsp}, @tinned tomatoes{400%g} and @beef stock{150%ml}. Stir once.
-- source: USDA Food Safety and Inspection Service, "Slow Cookers and Food Safety", revised February 2012, https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/GOVPUB-A110-PURL-gpo30229/pdf/GOVPUB-A110-PURL-gpo30229.pdf (vegetables cook more slowly than meat in a slow cooker, so they go in first)

> Why so little liquid: with the lid on there is almost no
> evaporation, so a sauce never reduces and concentrates the way it
> does in an oven. Start with less liquid than a stove recipe uses.
-- source: Scott Kathan, "Top Tips for Making Better Slow-Cooker Chicken, Stews, Sauces, and More", America's Test Kitchen, 2024-10-20, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/articles/5331-5-tips-for-making-more-delicious-food-in-your-slow-cooker

Put the lid on. Cook on high for ~{1%hour}, then on low for ~{7%hours}, until a fork slides into the beef with no resistance. Do not lift the lid to look.

> Why the lid stays on: every time it comes off, the cooker loses a
> lot of heat and the cooking time stretches.
-- source: Sarah Sandler and Lisa McManus, "The Best Slow Cookers", America's Test Kitchen, 2025-11-05, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/equipment-reviews/1731-slow-cookers ; USDA Food Safety and Inspection Service, "Slow Cookers and Food Safety", revised February 2012, https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/GOVPUB-A110-PURL-gpo30229/pdf/GOVPUB-A110-PURL-gpo30229.pdf ("Keep the lid in place")

> Science: a slow cooker works at roughly 77 to 138 °C. The test
> kitchen says good models top out at about 93 °C, with low getting
> there more slowly, though its own readings run from about 88 to
> 100 °C across models and settings. That is the band where collagen,
> the protein that makes cheap cuts tough, breaks down fastest: above
> about 82 °C and below a boil. Look up: collagen, gelatin, braising.
-- source: USDA Food Safety and Inspection Service, "Slow Cookers and Food Safety", revised February 2012, https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/GOVPUB-A110-PURL-gpo30229/pdf/GOVPUB-A110-PURL-gpo30229.pdf ("generally between 170 °F and 280 °F") ; Scott Kathan, "Top Tips for Making Better Slow-Cooker Chicken, Stews, Sauces, and More", America's Test Kitchen, 2024-10-20, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/articles/5331-5-tips-for-making-more-delicious-food-in-your-slow-cooker ("about 200 degrees") ; Andrew Janjigian, "The Science Behind Perfect Braised Brisket", America's Test Kitchen, 2017-11-01, https://www.americastestkitchen.com/articles/552-how-to-braise-brisket ("most rapidly above 180 degrees")

Stir in @tinned kidney beans{1%tin}(400 g, drained and rinsed) and cook for ~{30%minutes} more. If the chilli is thin, mash some of the beans against the side of the pot. Taste and adjust the salt.

> Safety: thaw meat fully before it goes in, since a slow cooker can
> take hours to reach a temperature that kills bacteria. If you can,
> run the first hour on high. If the power goes out while you are away,
> throw the food away, even if it looks done. Put leftovers in shallow
> containers and refrigerate them within two hours, and reheat them on
> the hob or in a microwave to 74 °C, not in the slow cooker.
-- source: USDA Food Safety and Inspection Service, "Slow Cookers and Food Safety", revised February 2012, https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/GOVPUB-A110-PURL-gpo30229/pdf/GOVPUB-A110-PURL-gpo30229.pdf

> Next time, without the recipe: a tough cut, browned in a pan; less
> liquid than you think; vegetables underneath; lid on and left alone;
> thicken at the end if it needs it.
